11. ROW LEVEL SECURITY AND STORAGE SECURITY
==================================================

Enable RLS on all exposed tables.

Provide complete SQL migrations with explicit policies.

Required policy principles:

- Anyone may read active categories and published template metadata.
- Anonymous users cannot download files.
- Authenticated users may read their own profile and update only approved profile fields.
- Users cannot modify their own roles.
- Users may read their own orders, order items, payment summaries, service requests, quotes, and download history as appropriate.
- Customers cannot mark orders paid.
- Customers cannot modify verified payment records.
- Customers cannot publish templates.
- Only trusted admins can manage published content, pricing, campaigns, and roles.
- Contributors can access only their own authorized submissions and approved records.
- Service request attachments must be private and owner/admin-authorized.
- Ad inquiries must not be publicly readable.
- Ad event writes must be protected against arbitrary campaign manipulation.
- Public users cannot access unpublished templates or private storage objects.
- Avoid trusting user-submitted role claims or metadata that users can change.
- Use secure server-side functions for admin-only actions and sensitive business logic.

Storage buckets:
- sheetora-files: PRIVATE.
- sheetora-previews: PUBLIC only for non-sensitive preview assets.
- sheetora-service-attachments: PRIVATE.
- sheetora-ad-creatives: choose a safe access strategy based on the public creative requirements.

Use opaque object paths and file size/type validation.

Provide SQL policies for Storage objects, not just database tables.

For protected downloads, validate ownership and entitlement before creating a signed URL. Use short expiration times.

If an administrative function uses service-role privileges, authenticate the caller and verify their admin role before performing the action.

